[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Why this should be correct and fast
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Correctness:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - out = 2*x + 3 elementwise matches the original expression.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Dtype preserved (float32 default). If you pass float16, it still computes elementwise in that dtype; results match PyTorch within dtype precision.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Performance:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Single pass, coalesced loads/stores; memory-bound and close to peak bandwidth for large tensors.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Autotune BLOCK_SIZE/num_warps gives reasonable occupancy across sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Limitations and extensions
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Only CUDA tensors are accelerated. CPU falls back to PyTorch.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype support: float32 by default. You can extend to fp16/bf16 by keeping operations in that dtype (as done) or upcasting to fp32 for math then downcasting (slightly different numerics).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If you had a chain of elementwise ops, you could fuse them into one Triton kernel to save memory traffic and launch overhead.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m This gives you a Triton kernel entry point ModelNew.forward that is a drop-in replacement for the provided operation, with GPU acceleration when available and a safe fallback otherwise. 
