[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The Model is just a thin wrapper around torch.cumsum(x, dim=self.dim).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - In your test setup, x has shape (32768, 32768) and dim=1, i.e., you’re doing a cumulative sum along the second axis for 32768 rows, each of length 32768.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Performance characteristics of torch.cumsum
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum is already a well-optimized CUDA kernel in PyTorch for many shapes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - However, it is a general-purpose implementation and may not be optimal for very specific, large, 1D-per-row scans with no跨行 dependencies.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Your workload is a perfect fit for a simple, dedicated scan kernel: independent rows, contiguous memory along the scan dimension, no branches, purely numeric.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) What Triton can do well here
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We can write a specialized kernel that does an inclusive scan (prefix sum) row by row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Memory access is contiguous and coalesced (each row is 1D contiguous in PyTorch), which is ideal.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We can choose block sizes and warps to match the problem size and GPU architecture.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Algorithm choices for prefix sum in Triton
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m There are two main approaches:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Block-wise sequential scan with loop: simple to implement, but has a Python-level loop over blocks; may be okay but not the most scalable.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Hillis–Steele style parallel scan within a block: Each lane at step j adds the value at position i ^ j (xor) shifted by j. This is O(log W) steps and vectorized; good for block-wide scan. However, to get the correct global prefix you need to know the block’s prefix sum (sum of all previous elements). You can compute that by a reduction, but then you either need to loop over blocks or do another kernel. It complicates things and is more code.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Given the shape here (N=32768), a block-wise sequential scan is perfectly fine:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use a block size like 1024 or 2048.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For each block, loop over elements and accumulate a running sum, then store results. No inter-lane dependencies.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid is (M,), one program per row. This keeps things simple and fast for this use case.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Numerical considerations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The input is torch.rand → float32. We can keep float32. If you later use float16, accumulation in float32 is better numerically; you can upcast to float32 for accumulation and downcast on store. For now, keep it simple with float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Stride and layout
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll honor arbitrary strides, but the fast path assumes contiguous last dimension (usual for PyTorch). We’ll pass stride(0) and stride(1) and compute addresses as base + i*stride1 + row*stride0.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If non-contiguous, it still works as long as the tensor is reasonably laid out; contiguous is best.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 7) Launch parameters
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid = (M,), where M = x.shape[0].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - BLOCK = 1024 or 2048. 1024 is a safe default; 2048 may be faster on some GPUs.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_warps: 4 or 8 is typical. We can start with 4.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Loop while i < N: i += BLOCK.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 8) Correctness
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Inclusive scan: out[j] = sum_{k=0..j} x[k].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The kernel computes that exactly per row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 9) Fallback
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If x is not on CUDA, fall back to torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 10) Expected performance
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For 32768 rows × 32768 elements, this is ~1e9 adds. A modern GPU should handle this in seconds.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The dedicated kernel removes some of the general-purpose overhead and should be competitive or faster, especially because the access is perfectly coalesced.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 11) Extensibility
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - You can later add block-wide parallel scan with inter-block prefix handling if needed.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - You can also support dim=0 by transposing or by writing a similar kernel that iterates over columns.
