[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m What the original code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The original PyTorch Model simply calls torch.cumsum(x, dim=self.dim). With the provided setup:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - batch_size = 32768
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - input_shape = (32768,)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - dim = 1
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - get_inputs returns a list containing a single tensor of shape (32768, 32768).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - So the operation is a cumulative sum along dim=1 for a very large 2D tensor: for each of the 32768 rows, compute the inclusive prefix sum over 32768 elements.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Observations about performance and memory
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum on CUDA is already highly optimized and uses efficient scan algorithms under the hood. Beating it generally requires very specific kernels and careful tuning.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The tensor size here is 32768 x 32768 = about 1.05e9 elements. If dtype is float32, that’s ~4 GB just for the tensor (not counting intermediate/temporary storage).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - A straightforward serial per-row scan would be memory-bandwidth bound and take a long time. The challenge is to parallelize the scan while keeping memory accesses coalesced and minimizing passes over data.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Algorithm choices for parallel prefix sum (scan)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - There are two broad categories:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   1) Blockwise serial scan with block-prefix accumulation:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Divide each row into blocks of size B.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Compute inclusive scan inside each block serially (or via a small unrolled loop) in registers.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Compute the block sums (sum of each block) in parallel via a vector reduce.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Compute exclusive prefix of block sums via a recursive/iterative scan (either serial loop of block sums, or another parallel scan).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Add the prefix sum of previous blocks to the current block values.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - This needs 2–3 passes over blocks but keeps most work register-local per block.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   2) Logarithmic-step Hillis–Steele style scan:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Represent the row as a flat vector.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Iteratively, for strides 1, 2, 4, … up to the largest power of two ≤ N:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m        - Load the value at position i−stride when i≥stride.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m        - Add it to当前位置的累加和。This typically requires multiple passes over the entire vector and global memory reads/writes per step.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For large N and many rows, blockwise method (1) is usually more cache/memory-friendly because each block’s inner scan is local, and block sums are a single reduction pass. The block-prefix computation can be done with a small number of steps.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Design for Triton kernel
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll implement the blockwise method.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid mapping:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - One Triton program instance per row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Each program scans its row in blocks of BLOCK size.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Within each program:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Loop over blocks 0..NUM_BLOCKS:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Load BLOCK contiguous elements for this block.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Compute inclusive scan of this block serially in registers: s[0]=x[0]; s[1]=s[0]+x[1]; … Store s to out.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - While loading, also accumulate the sum of this block in a scalar block_sum (or vector sum reduced to scalar).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - After finishing all blocks, we have, for each block b, its sum block_sums[b].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Compute exclusive prefix of block_sums: block_prefix[b] = sum of block_sums[0..b-1].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - This can be done via repeated additions in a small loop. Given NUM_BLOCKS is N/BLOCK, for N=32768 and BLOCK=1024, NUM_BLOCKS=32 — tiny.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Second pass over blocks:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - For each block, read its block_prefix and add to all values of that block in out (i.e., out[block,:] += block_prefix).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Memory:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - First pass: loads x, stores out.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Second pass: loads out block values and stores updated out.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - This is two full passes over data but keeps inner-block work register-local and coalesced.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Use float32 for simplicity (matches torch.rand default). If needed, this can be extended to half/bfloat16 by accumulating in float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Strides:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Use general strides so it works for non-contiguous tensors too (although here it will be contiguous).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tuning:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - BLOCK size: 1024 is a good starting point for register pressure and coalescing.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - num_warps: 4 or 8 are typical; 4 is fine here.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Correctness:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Inclusive scan: out[i] = sum_{j<=i} x[j] along dim.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The two-pass method produces correct results.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fallback:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - If tensor is on CPU, fall back to torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Potential limitations and notes
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This kernel is written for 2D tensors and dim=1 (as in your setup). It can be generalized, but keeping it simple reduces risk.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For such large tensors, ensure you have enough GPU memory; the example tensor is ~4 GB in float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum may still be faster due to vendor-optimized implementations; this Triton version is educational and shows how to structure a blockwise scan.
