[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - It defines a model whose forward computes torch.matmul(A.T, B).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Shapes: A is (K, M), B is (K, N), so A.T is (M, K). The result C = A.T @ B is (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - In terms of indices, C[m, n] = sum_k A.T[m, k] * B[k, n] = sum_k A[k, m] * B[k, n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - So it’s a (K, M) x (K, N) = (M, N) product if you interpret it as (M, K) x (K, N), but written as A.T @ B.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Observations about performance and memory access
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A is (K, M) with contiguous rows; B is (K, N) with contiguous rows.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - If we compute C = A.T @ B directly, we’d read A with stride M (non-contiguous along K) which is not ideal for coalesced loads.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A better memory pattern is to use A^T logically without materializing it: access A as (k, m) and B as (k, n), which are both contiguous along their second dimension when the tensor is contiguous. This is the standard matmul tiling pattern: iterate k in blocks, and for each tile, accumulate A[m, k] * B[k, n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - That means the Triton kernel should implement:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - C[m0:m0+BM, n0:n0+BN] = sum over k0:k0+BK of A[m0:m0+BM, k0:k0+BK] * B[k0:k0+BK, n0:n0+BN]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - With strides reflecting the actual tensor strides (don’t assume contiguous other than typical).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype considerations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The provided get_inputs uses torch.rand defaults to float32, so float32 is fine.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - We can start with float32 to keep the kernel simple (no need for fp16/bf16 accumulation nuance).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - If needed later, we can extend to fp16/bf16 with fp32 accumulation.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tiling and parameters
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Shapes are large: M ≈ 2048, K ≈ 8192, N ≈ 4096. A good starting point:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - BLOCK_M = 64 or 128
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - BLOCK_N = 64 or 128
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - BLOCK_K = 32 or 64
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - num_warps = 4–8, num_stages = 3–4 are typical starting points.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - We’ll choose BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64, BLOCK_K=32, num_warps=4, num_stages=3 as a balanced default. These are conservative and work across many GPUs.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid and bounds
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Grid = (ceil_div(M, BM), ceil_div(N, BN)).
