[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code computes
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The original forward returns torch.matmul(A.T, B).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Given get_inputs() returns A shape (K, M) and B shape (K, N), A.T has shape (M, K).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So the result C has shape (M, N), with C = A^T @ B.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Elementwise: C[m, n] = sum over k of A[k, m] * B[k, n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Mathematical equivalence and shape implications
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This is equivalent to C = (A @ B^T)^T:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Let C' = A @ B^T. Then C' shape is (K, K) if you naively look at (K,M) @ (K,N), but actually B^T has shape (N, K), so (K,M) @ (N,K) is only valid if N == M. Wait: that’s not right.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Correction: B is (K, N), so B^T is (N, K). Then A (K, M) @ B^T (N, K) is invalid unless M == N and K == K. So A @ B^T is valid when M == N, and output is (K, K).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - But A.T @ B is valid for any M, N: (M, K) @ (K, N) -> (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Therefore, the most faithful and general implementation is to compute C = A^T @ B directly with shapes (M, K) x (K, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Root cause of the previous failure and fix
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Previous attempt implemented a naive elementwise add kernel and asserted x.shape == y.shape. The harness passed A (K,M) and B (K,N) with M != N, so it crashed.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fix: implement a proper matmul kernel that respects the shapes (M, K) x (K, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Also fix the Triton matmul kernel bug: tl.dot shape requirements are (BLOCK_M, BLOCK_K) @ (BLOCK_K, BLOCK_N). Previously I loaded A as (BLOCK_K, BLOCK_M) and B as (BLOCK_K, BLOCK_N) and used tl.dot on those, which violated the requirement because the second dimension of the first must equal the first dimension of the second. So use a = A[m, k] and b = B[k, n] tile shapes (BLOCK_M, BLOCK_K) and (BLOCK_K, BLOCK_N).
