[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The Model class wraps torch.cumsum(x, dim=self.dim).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The provided shapes are very large: batch_size = 32768 and input_shape = (32768,), so the tensor has shape (32768, 32768) → 1,073,741,824 elements.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Cumulative sum (prefix sum) along dim=1 means: for each of the 32,768 rows, compute the inclusive prefix sum of the 32,768 floats in that row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Where the cost is
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum is already highly optimized in PyTorch (it dispatches to efficient CUDA kernels or uses Thrust). For such large tensors, it will likely be very fast.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - However, a custom Triton kernel can be competitive or sometimes faster if we tailor memory access and parallelism to this specific pattern:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Access is contiguous along the scan dimension (dim=1), which is good for coalesced loads/stores.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The scan length per row is 32,768, a power of two, which is friendly for block-wise algorithms.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Algorithm choice for Triton
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Inclusive scan (cumsum) is a scan operation. There are two main approaches:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   1) Hillis–Steele style in-register scan using powers of two and vector shifting. This is elegant but requires shifting vectors by arbitrary amounts; Triton doesn’t expose a simple “shift a vector” primitive that works for arbitrary offsets in a single step.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   2) Block-wise inclusive scan per tile, then carry propagation between tiles. This is straightforward with Triton’s programming model:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Step A: For each row, process it in tiles of BLOCK_SIZE.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Step B: Within each tile, do an in-place inclusive scan. We can use a simple O(B^2) add of “running sum + data shifted by k” for k in 1..B-1. For BLOCK_SIZE=1024, that’s 1,023 vector additions — acceptable for a tile.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m      - Step C: Maintain a running “carry” equal to the last value of the previous tile. Add this carry to all elements of the current tile after the tile scan.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Why this works and its performance
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Memory access: Loads and stores are contiguous and coalesced along the scan dimension. That’s optimal.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Computation: Within a tile, the O(B^2) method is simple and vectorized. For B=1024, it’s about a million flops per tile. Across 32 tiles per row, ~32 MFLOPs per row; across 32k rows, ~1 TFLOP — well within GPU capability.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Parallelism: We parallelize over rows (program_id(0) = row index). Each program processes its row sequentially in tiles; this is a common pattern for scan-like ops.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Numerics: We’ll use float32. The order of additions is different from torch.cumsum (tile-then-carry), but still reasonable. If needed, we could keep a scalar carry and only adjust the first element of each tile, but that would require a special first-step or more complex logic; the simple carry addition is typically fine.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Design details and edge cases
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dim handling: The provided use case is dim=1. Supporting arbitrary dims would require more indexing gymnastics. For simplicity and performance, we’ll implement the fast path for dim=1 on 2D tensors. If dim != 1 or ndim != 2, we’ll fallback to torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype: We’ll assume float32. If not float32, we can either convert or fallback. Here we’ll fallback to torch.cumsum for non-float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Device: Triton requires CUDA. If tensor is on CPU, fallback.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Strides: We’ll honor arbitrary strides (not just contiguous), by passing stride0 and stride1. But for best performance, contiguous is preferred.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - BLOCK_SIZE: 1024 is a good default for 32,768. You can tune it (512, 2048) depending on register pressure and GPU.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_warps/num_stages: 4–8 warps is fine. We’ll start with 4.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Potential further optimizations (not implemented here to keep code clear)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use a more sophisticated in-register scan (log2(B) steps) if Triton exposes convenient vector manipulation.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Process multiple rows per program to improve occupancy, at the cost of more complex code.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use shared memory-like patterns within a program to keep data resident; Triton’s block pointers help but the pattern is non-trivial for scans.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Mixed-precision: do accumulation in float32 even if input is float16, then cast back.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Correctness considerations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The block-wise scan plus carry produces the same result as a global scan: each tile is scanned inclusively, then we add the sum of all preceding elements (the carry) to the whole tile. This matches torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Masking: If N is not a multiple of BLOCK_SIZE, the last tile is masked; the tile scan algorithm still works because out = cumsum + carry turns into out = cumsum'(valid) + carry, which is correct for valid lanes.
