[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Support arbitrary dim? The example uses 2D and dim=1. Supporting arbitrary dim in a general, fast way is doable (move target dim to last, flatten others, scan last dim, reshape back), but adds complexity. I’ll implement an efficient 2D kernel for dim=1 first, then generalize to arbitrary dim by reshaping.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Dtypes: torch.rand defaults to float32. We can start with float32. Extending to float16/bfloat16 is possible but needs care with accumulation precision.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Algorithm choice:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Hillis–Steele style inclusive scan in blocks:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - For each row, iterate over blocks of size BLOCK_N.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Within a block, load values, compute inclusive scan using iterative doubling:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m       y = 0; for offset in [1, 2, 4, …, BLOCK_N): y = y + x; x = x + y
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Keep a running carry equal to the sum of previous blocks; add carry to the block result.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Store results.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - This uses only local vector registers, no global synchronization, and is simple and fast for many sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Parallelism:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Grid = (num_rows, num_blocks_along_N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Each program instance handles one row and one block.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Memory access:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - We ensure the tensor is contiguous; strides are simple.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Loads/stores are coalesced along the N dimension.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Block size:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Choose BLOCK_N as a power-of-two up to a cap (e.g., 1024 or 2048), close to N if possible.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Number of iterations is log2(BLOCK_N), which is small.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Edge cases and correctness:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Masking for tail when N is not a multiple of BLOCK_N.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Carry propagation: the carry is the sum of all previous blocks in the row; after each block we update carry = carry + local_block_sum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Inclusive scan: the loop above produces inclusive scan because each element x[i] gets y = y + x[i] then x = x + y, so x[i] becomes x[i] + sum(x[0..i-1]).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype and precision:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Start with float32 to match torch.rand default.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - If needed, we can extend to float16 by accumulating in float32 and casting back.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Generalization to arbitrary dim:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Moveaxis the target dim to the last axis: x_m = x.moveaxis(dim, -1).contiguous().
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - View as 2D: (M, N) where N is size along dim, M is product of other dims.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Run the kernel row-wise on (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Moveaxis back to original dim position.
