[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Explanation of fixes and performance
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fixed the root cause: implemented cumsum instead of logsumexp and returned the correct 2D shape matching input.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Kernel computes inclusive cumsum along the last dimension for each row:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Processes columns in blocks.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Uses tl.cumsum for vectorized block scan (available in recent Triton); adds carry from previous blocks; updates carry by block sum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Masks tail; computes in float32 for numeric stability, then stores in input dtype (torch.empty_like preserves dtype).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fallback to torch.cumsum if Triton or CUDA is not available.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Performance:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Coalesced memory access along last dimension.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Parallel across R rows.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - BLOCK sizes 256–1024, num_warps 4 is a good starting point on A100.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Limitations:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Relies on tl.cumsum; if your Triton version lacks it, the kernel won’t compile. You can replace the block scan part with a correct but slower serial loop (O(BLOCK^2) per block) for correctness at a performance cost.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Focus on floating tensors; integer cumsum is possible with identical code but may have overflow considerations.
