[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Performance characteristics and constraints
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Problem size: 32768 × 32768 ≈ 1.07B elements. At float32 that’s ~4.27 GB for the output alone (plus input). This is huge and will be memory-bandwidth bound.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum is already optimized, but a custom Triton kernel can:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Fuse memory access and avoid framework overhead.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Parallelize across rows.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Use vectorized loads/stores.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - However, writing a fast parallel prefix-sum (scan) kernel is non-trivial. Naive approaches either:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Do a serial scan per row (easy but slow: O(N) per row in Python, not GPU-parallel).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Do a block-wise scan with inter-block dependency (more complex; needs either multiple passes or smart in-place updates).
