[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Optimization approach: block-wise Hillis–Steele scan with carry
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Map programs over rows: one Triton program per row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Process the row in fixed-size tiles of BLOCK elements.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For each tile:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Load the tile from x into registers.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Perform an in-register inclusive scan using the Hillis–Steele algorithm:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     for d in 0..LOG2(BLOCK)-1: v = v + v shifted right by 2^d
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     This is O(log BLOCK) vector ops and very efficient.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Store the scanned tile to y.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The last valid element of the scanned tile is the carry to add to the next tile.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Before the scan, add a per-row carry (from previous tiles) to all positions so the scan result is correctly offset.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This gives:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Each element loaded once, stored once.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - O(N log BLOCK) vector ops total.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - No global memory dependencies within a tile; simple to implement and fast.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Key implementation details
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use BLOCK as a power of two (e.g., 1024 or 2048 or 4096). 1024 is a good starting point: LOG2=10.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use tl.arange to get column indices within a tile.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use masks for out-of-bounds on the last tile.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The tricky part is the right-shift (gather from previous positions). Triton makes this easy via tensor ops:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Create an index tensor idx = cols - shift with modulo handling for out-of-range lanes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Use tl.where to select between vals at idx (for in-range lanes) and 0 (for out-of-range).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This avoids dynamic indexing and keeps everything vectorized.
