[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The Model class simply wraps torch.cumsum(x, dim=self.dim).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - In the provided setup:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - batch_size = 32768
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - input_shape = (32768,)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - dim = 1
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So the input tensor x has shape (32768, 32768), and the operation is a cumulative sum along dim=1 (i.e., per-row prefix sum across 32768 elements).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Computational characteristics
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Number of elements: 32768 × 32768 = 1,073,741,824 elements.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If dtype is float32: ~4 GB of memory for x, another ~4 GB for output = ~8 GB just for data. Computation is memory-bound.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum likely uses an efficient library (e.g., cuDNN/cuBLAS/cuPrefixSum) on CUDA, but still memory-bound. A custom Triton kernel can be competitive if we minimize memory traffic and keep accesses coalesced.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) What a naive Triton kernel would do and why it’s not ideal
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Naive approach: assign one Triton “program” per row, and within that program iterate over columns sequentially accumulating a running sum and storing outputs. That is essentially a serial loop per row inside a single program.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Pros: very simple, minimal synchronization.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Cons: low parallelism within a row; you lose vectorization along the long axis; might underutilize the GPU, especially for large rows. Also, the loop has to be dynamic, which is less friendly to Triton’s compile-time unrolling.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Better approach: block-wise parallel prefix-sum (scan) within a row
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Split each row into blocks of size BLOCK_N.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Kernel step 1 (inclusive scan within blocks):
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For each row, for each block, compute the inclusive prefix sum of that block’s values. This can be vectorized within the block.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Store each block’s sum (the sum of its values, equal to its last element for an inclusive scan) into a “block_sums” array.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Kernel step 2 (exclusive scan of block sums across blocks per row):
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Compute the exclusive prefix sums of the block sums array along the block dimension for each row. That gives you the offset to add to all elements of each block (except the first block, which gets 0).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Kernel step 3 (add offsets):
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Add the per-block offset (from step 2) to each element of the corresponding block (from step 1).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Why this helps:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Step 1 is parallel within blocks; memory accesses are coalesced (contiguous loads/stores).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Step 2 is a 1D scan over relatively small length B = ceil(N / BLOCK_N) per row; can be done efficiently with a standard iterative-doubling scan algorithm.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Step 3 is a simple vector add.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Algorithmic details
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Inclusive scan within a block:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Let v be the vector of BLOCK_N values.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Running = 0
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For j in 0..BLOCK_N-1:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - out[j] = Running + v[j]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Running += v[j]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - This is vectorizable: at each iteration, we load v[j], then compute out = out + running; running = running + v; but we need elementwise assignment to out[j]. Easiest and safe is a loop; Triton supports Python for-loops in kernels; BLOCK_N is a constexpr, so the loop is unrolled at compile time.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Exclusive scan of block sums (1D along B):
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - s is length-B vector of block sums.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Output is length-B vector of prefixes p.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Iterative doubling:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - p = 0
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - step = 1
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - while step < B:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m       - tmp = p  (keep a copy of previous p)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m       - p = p + s[step + pid] with mask (index < B), using the latest p where index < step
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m       - Requires shifting p by step: construct p_shift = where(index >= step, p, 0); then p = p + where(index < B, tmp, 0)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m       - This avoids races because for each index, the value it reads comes from the previous iteration’s p (tmp), not the current p.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - After the loop, p contains exclusive scan results.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Then we write out inclusive scan by adding s: out_block_sum_inclusive = p + s.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Add offsets:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For each block, load its offset p[block_idx], then add to every element of that block: y = y + offset; store.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Memory layout and strides
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll assume row-major contiguous input; but to be safe, we’ll pass strides and use them.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Shape is 2D: (M, N). dim=1 means scan along N for each of M rows.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Pointer arithmetic: row base is row_id * stride_row; col offset is col * stride_col.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 7) Dtype and precision
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The example uses torch.rand => float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll implement for float32 to keep it simple and numerically stable.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Could be extended to float16/bfloat16 with accumulation in float32, but that’s more code.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 8) BLOCK_N choice
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - BLOCK_N should be a power of two for clean vector ops and loop unrolling.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Reasonable starting points: 1024 or 2048 or 4096.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Larger BLOCK_N increases work per program, smaller increases number of blocks B.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Given N=32768, BLOCK_N=2048 gives B=16; BLOCK_N=1024 gives B=32. Both are fine.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll start with BLOCK_N=2048.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 9) Grid and launch
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid = (M,) one program per row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_warps: 4 or 8 is typical; we can start with 4.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_stages: 2 is fine.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 10) Correctness and edge cases
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Last block might be partially filled: in step 1, we load with mask and other=0, so the algorithm remains correct (zeros don’t change the sum).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If N < BLOCK_N: B=1; scan algorithms退化成 trivial.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Non-CUDA: fallback to torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 11) Performance expectations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This is memory-bound; the block-scan approach should be competitive with torch.cumsum because:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Coalesced loads/stores.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   -大部分 work is simple additions.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Only three passes over data (two small, one big).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - cuDNN/cu* routines are very optimized; but this custom kernel can match or beat for specific shapes, especially where we can tune BLOCK_N and warps.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 12) Potential further optimizations (not implemented here to keep clarity)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fuse steps or use more advanced in-register scan algorithms ( Hillis–Steele ) with careful indexing.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Process multiple rows per program to improve occupancy if M is small.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Vectorize across both row and column to better utilize SIMD width.
