[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - It wraps torch.cumsum along a given dimension.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - With the provided shapes: batch_size = 32768 and input_shape = (32768,), the input tensor has shape (32768, 32768), and dim = 1.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So it computes a cumulative sum along the second dimension (columns) for each of the 32768 rows.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Performance characteristics of torch.cumsum
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.cumsum is already quite optimized in PyTorch, but it’s a general-purpose implementation that handles arbitrary shapes/dtypes and arbitrary dim.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For very large 1D scans or scans along a single axis in a 2D tensor, a specialized GPU kernel can outperform the general implementation, especially if we can ensure coalesced memory access and fuse operations.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The given problem size is substantial: 32768 x 32768 elements ≈ 1.07 billion elements. If dtype is float32, that’s ~4.28 GB of data. Even float16 is ~2.14 GB. This is large but doable on modern GPUs.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Constraints and goals for a Triton implementation
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We need to implement prefix sum (cumulative sum) along an axis.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - In the given setup, axis = 1 (columns) of a 2D tensor.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We should preserve dtype (float32, float16, bfloat16) and device.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Maintain numerical behavior as close as possible to torch.cumsum (left-to-right scan, no reordering).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Ensure memory access is coalesced: contiguous loads/stores along the scan axis help.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Handle arbitrary strides (non-contiguous) if possible, but for performance we’ll prefer contiguous.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Parallelize across rows: one program per row is a good start; each program computes the cumulative sum for its row.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Algorithm choices
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - There are two main classes of parallel scan algorithms:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   a) Block-wise scan with upsweep/downsweep (Blelloch or Hillis-Steele style), which parallelizes within blocks using logarithmic steps and block carries. This is complex to implement efficiently in a custom kernel, especially with variable block sizes and mixed dtypes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   b) Simple sequential loop per row, but vectorized in blocks: load a block of elements, compute sequential inclusive scan in registers, store, repeat. This is easier, leverages GPU parallelism across rows, and is often good enough for many workloads.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Given the simplicity and the size, a block-wise sequential approach inside each program is a pragmatic first step:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Process the row in chunks of BLOCK_SIZE elements.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For each chunk: load up to BLOCK_SIZE elements, do a left-to-right accumulation in a register (running sum), store each result. This ensures numerical parity with torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Loop over chunks until the whole row is done.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This approach is memory-coalesced (contiguous loads/stores), simple, and avoids tricky inter-thread communication within a block.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Memory layout and strides
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Assume x is 2D: shape (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll support arbitrary strides (not just contiguous) by passing stride0 and stride1.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - But for performance, we’ll make x contiguous before launching the kernel. That gives stride1 = 1 and stride0 = N, simplifying addressing and maximizing coalescing.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Dtype handling
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll support float32, float16, bfloat16.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Accumulation can be done in the same dtype to match torch.cumsum behavior exactly. Using higher-precision accumulation (fp32 for fp16) would change numerical results slightly; we’ll keep same dtype accumulation for parity.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 7) Kernel configuration
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid: one program per row => grid = (M,).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - BLOCK_SIZE: a power-of-two like 256 or 512 or 1024 are good starting points. 256 is a safe default that balances register pressure and occupancy.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_warps: 4 or 8 are typical. We can start with 4.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 8) Correctness and edge cases
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Masking for tail: when N is not a multiple of BLOCK_SIZE, mask the last load and just store the accumulated values; no “other” value needed because we use running sum and store each step.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Negative dims: normalize dim.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - CPU fallback: if tensor is not CUDA, fall back to torch.cumsum.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Non-2D input: the provided setup is 2D. We can assert 2D or extend later.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 9) Expected performance
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This kernel parallelizes across rows; each row is processed sequentially in blocks. It should be competitive for large M and moderate N.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For very large N, a more advanced in-block parallel scan could help, but is significantly more complex. As a starting point, this should be fast and correct.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
