[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Why this should fix the prior issues and be correct
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The kernel no longer uses illegal tensor indexing inside JIT; all loads/stores are scalar-address based, which Triton supports.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The accumulation is strictly left-to-right in the tensor’s dtype, matching torch.cumsum’s numerical behavior.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Memory access is coalesced along columns for contiguous tensors.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fallbacks ensure correctness on CPU or unsupported shapes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Performance notes and possible extensions
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This kernel parallelizes across rows; within each row, the scan is sequential but keeps correctness. For the problem size (32768 x 32768), having 32768 concurrent programs is sufficient to saturate the GPU.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If you need more speed and can tolerate tiny numerical differences, you could implement an in-block vectorized scan (e.g., Hillis-Steele using power-of-two steps) and carry between blocks. That is more complex and changes operation order, so I’ve kept the strictly ordered version here to preserve correctness. 
