[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m What changed and why it fixes the issue
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Removed the use of tl.tanh which caused the compile-time crash. Replaced with a numerically stable tanh implementation using exp: tanh(x) = (1 - e^{-2x}) / (1 + e^{-2x}).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Kept the 5D indexing consistent with NCDHW layout after mean, using element strides.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Ensured softmax is computed in float32 for stability, bias added before softmax, and results cast back to input dtype.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fused operations to reduce memory traffic and kernel launches for a clear speedup over the original PyTorch tail.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Potential further improvements
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tune BLOCK_C, num_warps, num_stages for your GPU and problem sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If C is large, consider a two-level reduction or BLOCK_C = C to reduce loop overhead.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Advanced: fuse mean over depth into the same kernel by looping over D and accumulating, but that’s a bigger change and not necessary to get speedups here. 
