[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The forward computes torch.matmul(A.T, B) where:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A has shape (K, M)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B has shape (K, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - A.T has shape (M, K), so the matmul is (M, K) @ (K, N) = (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Result C[i, j] = sum_{k=0..K-1} A[k, i] * B[k, j].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - In terms of memory:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A is row-major (K, M): contiguous along M (last dim). Strides: sA0 = M, sA1 = 1.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B is row-major (K, N): contiguous along N (last dim). Strides: sB0 = N, sB1 = 1.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A.T (M, K) would be strides sAT0 = 1, sAT1 = K if created as a view; but that view is not contiguous and has a non-trivial stride on the first dimension. If you materialize it contiguous, you’d get sC0 = K, sC1 = 1. Either way, using A.T in matmul means the first operand has K as its “running” dimension; PyTorch’s backend is quite good and will handle strides, but the shape (M, K) means the K dimension is the inner loop in some decompositions.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Opportunity for optimization with Triton
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The core operation is a dense GEMM: C = A @ B with A shape (M, K) and B shape (K, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Triton is a good fit for writing a custom matmul kernel that:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Tiles C into blocks of size (BLOCK_M, BLOCK_N), and loops over K in blocks of BLOCK_K.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Loads A and B tiles, does dot product accumulation in registers, and stores the result.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Uses masks to handle edge cases when dimensions aren’t multiples of block sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Performance considerations:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Use BLOCK sizes that are multiples of 16/32 for good memory coalescing and alignment.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Accumulate in float32 for numerical stability even if inputs are float16/bfloat16.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Choose num_warps and num_stages to balance occupancy and memory latency hiding.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Ensure loads are coalesced: for A[m, k] and B[k, n], both have contiguous last dimension, so loads along k and n should be coalesced.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Shape mapping and indexing
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Define A as (M, K), B as (K, N), C as (M, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For a tile (m : BLOCK_M, n : BLOCK_N), and loop over k in steps of BLOCK_K:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A_tile = A[m : m + BLOCK_M, k : k + BLOCK_K]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B_tile = B[k : k + BLOCK_K, n : n + BLOCK_N]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - C_tile += dot(A_tile, B_tile)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Pointer arithmetic:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A is (M, K) with strides (sAM, sAK); typical sAK = 1, sAM = K if contiguous.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B is (K, N) with strides (sBK, sBN); typical sBN = 1, sBK = N if contiguous.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - We’ll pass arbitrary strides to be robust.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Dtype and accumulation
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The provided get_inputs uses torch.rand defaults to float32, so we can start with float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We can still write the kernel to accumulate in float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For maximum performance, float16 or bfloat16 inputs with float32 accumulation are typical; but to keep this simple and correct, we’ll assume float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Grid, blocks, and launch
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid = (ceil_div(M, BLOCK_M), ceil_div(N, BLOCK_N)).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - BLOCK_M, BLOCK_N, BLOCK_K are constexpr parameters; typical starting values: 64, 128, 32 or 64.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - num_warps: 4 or 8 is a reasonable start; num_stages: 2–4.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll pick BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=128, BLOCK_K=32, num_warps=4, num_stages=3 as a baseline.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Correctness and edge cases
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Masks for boundary conditions when M, N, K are not multiples of block sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Support arbitrary strides (no need to make tensors contiguous; but making them contiguous can help).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Device: Triton requires CUDA; if tensors are on CPU, either move to CUDA or fall back.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 7) Expected performance
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For sizes M=2048, K=8192, N=4096:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - FLOPs ~ 2M*K*N = 2*2048*8192*4096 ≈ 1.3e14 flops → hundreds of GFLOPs.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - cuBLAS is very strong; a hand-rolled Triton kernel may be competitive but likely not beat cuBLAS out of the box without tuning.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The goal here is to demonstrate a clean Triton GEMM kernel, not necessarily to outperform cuBLAS immediately.
