[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Optimization opportunities and constraints:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Elementwise ops are almost always memory-bandwidth bound; the main win comes from:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Fewer reads/writes (fusion): read inputs once, compute, write once.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Fewer kernel launches and intermediate temporaries.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Here, the biggest cost is memory bandwidth and kernel launch overhead; Triton helps when we reduce Python overhead, fuse ops, and control tiling.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Key observations and strategy
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The original model’s core computation is a sequence of simple elementwise math and reductions:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - inv_std = 1.0 / torch.sqrt(var + eps)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - scale = weight * inv_std
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - y = x * weight + bias
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This is a classic map over large tensors. PyTorch will do separate passes, each reading x from memory and writing results back. A Triton kernel can fuse the math and memory traffic into one pass over the data.
