[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code actually computes
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The model is very simple:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - y = self.matmul(x)           -> y = x @ W^T + b
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - original_x = y.clone().detach()
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - y = y * scaling_factor
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - y = y + original_x
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Algebraically: y = y * s + y = y * (1 + s) = (x @ W^T + b) * (1 + s).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So the sequence clone → scale → add is redundant; it just multiplies the whole output by (1 + s).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Where time is spent
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The GEMM (matmul) dominates for these sizes: M=16384, K=4096, N=4096.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - FLOPs ~ 16384*4096*4096 ≈ 2.7e11 MACs.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The elementwise sequence (clone, scale, add) is minor but adds:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - clone: full read+write
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - scale: full read+write
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - add  : full read+write
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So the main win is to eliminate those elementwise passes and fold the scaling into the GEMM’s accumulator before adding bias.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Best practical optimization
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Highest-value change with minimal complexity: use torch.nn.functional.linear with a pre-scaled bias.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Let alpha = 1 + scaling_factor.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - out = F.linear(x, W, bias * alpha)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - This fuses matmul + bias add in cuBLAS/cuBLASLt and removes the extra elementwise passes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This is typically faster than a custom Triton GEMM for such large, general shapes unless you invest in a very advanced Triton kernel (TensorCore, swizzling, persistence). And it preserves correctness and gradients.
