[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Notes, trade-offs, and next steps
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Performance: For these large shapes, cuBLAS (torch.matmul) is very hard to beat in pure FP32. The Triton kernel here is didactic and structured, uses tiling, and avoids Python overhead, but will likely be similar or slower than PyTorch’s highly optimized elementwise kernels for tiny tensors because:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - torch.nn.functional.gaussian_nll_loss uses a custom backward kernel implemented in Triton. The loss function remains identical, but the backward pass for the quadratic term is implemented by a Triton kernel and reused in backward
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Keep numerics stable by using float32 accumulation internally even for half inputs (cast to float32 for accumulation), and cast back to the original dtype at store time.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Reduce memory traffic: Instead of writing intermediate results to global memory, keep partial accumulations in registers.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Favor coalesceded access patterns: For elementwise kernels, ensure contiguous memory access and coalesced loads. For strided tensors, pass explicit strides and indices to the kernel, and optionally prepack data to contiguous buffers before the kernel.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use a single fused pass: You can fuse several elementwise ops into one pass. This reduces memory traffic and kernel launch overhead.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Correctness and numerical stability:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - The fused kernel computes the same result as PyTorch’s F.linear with bias (y = x @ w^T + b), but in a single pass and without materializing intermediate tensors. This reduces memory traffic by:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m -   performing the dot product in a fused way with the transpose, memory-bound kernels: If the original PyTorch model uses standard PyTorch operations (like matmul, addmm, etc.) you can replace those with Triton kernels that reduce memory traffic and exploit the mathematically-structured memory access in GPUs. Here, instead of replacing softmax with a custom Triton kernel, you could fuse the following pattern:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Replace x[:, None] - x[None, :] (broadcast subtract) with a single fused kernel that computes numerator and exponent without allocating large broadcasted intermediates, reducing memory traffic and kernel launches.
