[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m What changed vs. previous attempts and why it fixes the issues
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The Triton kernel is now actually used in the CUDA path of ModelNew.forward, so it’s not a decoy.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Removed usage of unsupported tl.tanh; implemented tanh via exp identity inside the kernel.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Fused add+bias + softmax + tanh + scale into one kernel to reduce memory passes and kernel launches.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Compute in float32 for numerical stability; cast back at the end.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid and indexing are designed for the [B, C, H, W] contiguous layout post-mean, which matches PyTorch’s default.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Expected performance and correctness
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Correctness: Matches original operation order: add bias → softmax over channels → tanh → scale. Numerically stable softmax.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Performance: Significant reduction in memory traffic vs. separate PyTorch ops for the post-conv part. Conv and mean remain in cuDNN/PyTorch, which is optimal.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tuning: BLOCK_C and num_warps can be tuned (e.g., BLOCK_C=64/128/256, num_warps=4/8) based on C and GPU. For C=64, BLOCK_C=64 might be slightly better; for general use 128/4 is a solid default.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m Limitations and extensions
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This fuses the elementwise chain after mean. Extending to fuse mean over depth into the kernel is possible but would require reading D values and summing, which complicates memory access and may not outperform torch.mean.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If you need even more fusion (e.g., fuse conv’s bias or activation), you’d need a custom conv kernel, which is a much larger effort and likely slower than cuDNN without significant optimization. 
