[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - It defines Model.forward(A, B) = torch.matmul(A.T, B).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Shapes:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A is (K, M)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B is (K, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A.T is (M, K)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Result C = (A.T) @ B should be shape (M, N), with elements
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     C[i, j] = sum_{k=0..K-1} A[k, i] * B[k, j]
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Note: This is not the usual (M, K) @ (K, N) = (M, N). Here both inputs are transposed relative to that: A and B are (K, M) and (K, N), so you multiply (M, K) @ (K, N).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Performance characteristics and why Triton can help
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - torch.matmul will use cuBLAS and is highly optimized, but:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - There may be no fusion with subsequent ops if you plan to chain; here we are isolated, so cuBLAS is fine.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Triton can let us handcraft tiling and memory access patterns to match your exact layout and fuse epilogue ops if needed.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For these particular sizes (M=2048, K=8192, N=4096), a well-tuned FP32 matmul kernel in Triton can get close to cuBLAS, sometimes slightly better depending on hardware and tuning.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Memory layout:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A is (K, M) contiguous in the last dimension → stride_am = 1, stride_ak = M.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B is (K, N) contiguous in the last dimension → stride_bn = 1, stride_bk = N.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - We will read A[k, i] and B[k, j]; those are contiguous along K but strided along M/N. Our kernel will use BLOCK_K along K for coalesced loads and loop over it.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Correctness check: equivalence of shapes and formula
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Let A' = A.T with shape (M, K): A'[i, k] = A[k, i].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - C[i, j] = sum_k A'[i, k] * B[k, j] = sum_k A[k, i] * B[k, j].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So the formula is correct as written.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Dtype considerations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The example uses torch.rand → float32 by default.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll implement float32 first (simple and accurate). Extensions to float16/bfloat16 are possible (using FP32 accumulation) but add code complexity.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll add a CPU fallback and a non-CUDA fallback to torch.matmul.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Kernel structure (high level)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tile the output into blocks of size (BLOCK_M, BLOCK_N), loop k in steps of BLOCK_K.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For each tile:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Accumulate into a float32 accumulator acc[BM, BN] = 0.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - For kk from 0 to K step BLOCK_K:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Load A_sub [BLOCK_K, BLOCK_M] at (k=kk:m=offs_m) with proper masks.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - Load B_sub [BLOCK_K, BLOCK_N] at (k=kk:n=offs_n) with proper masks.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m     - acc += dot(A_sub.T, B_sub) → shape [BM, BN].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Store acc to C with mask for boundary.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use masks to handle non-multiple-of-block sizes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Tiling and parameters
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Good starting configs:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - BLOCK_M = 128, BLOCK_N = 128, BLOCK_K = 32 or 64
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - num_warps = 4 or 8, num_stages = 2 or 3
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Given M=2048, N=4096, K=8192:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - 128x128 tiles are a solid baseline.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - BLOCK_K=32 is a good start; you can try 64.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll provide a simple config and allow easy tuning.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 7) Memory access and coalescing
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - A[k, i] loads: last dimension K is contiguous (stride 1) so loading vectors along K is coalesced.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - B[k, j] loads: same, K contiguous.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The dot product uses those vectors; Triton will generate efficient code.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 8) Fusion
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - There’s no activation or bias here; if you had, you could fuse in the epilogue.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 9) Fallbacks and robustness
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If tensors are not CUDA, fall back to torch.matmul.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Ensure contiguous or use strides; we’ll use strides directly so non-contiguous is fine as long as they’re dense.
