[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - It defines a very simple model whose forward computes torch.matmul(A.T, B).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Shapes:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A: (K, M)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B: (K, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A.T: (M, K)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Result C = A.T @ B: shape (M, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - So it’s a standard GEMM: C[m, n] = sum over k of A_T[m, k] * B[k, n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Equivalent to sum over k of A[k, m] * B[k, n], because A_T[m, k] = A[k, m].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Why Triton can help
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - PyTorch will dispatch to cuBLAS/cuBLASLt for matmul, which is very fast, but sometimes custom kernels can do better by:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Fusing operations (not applicable here, since it’s a single matmul).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Tailoring tiling to problem sizes and dtypes.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Avoiding framework overhead when called repeatedly in tight loops.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Triton lets us write a simple, high-performance matmul kernel with explicit tiling and memory access patterns, and accumulate in FP32 for numerical stability when inputs are FP16/BF16.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) Constraints and choices
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Device: Triton targets GPUs; if tensors are on CPU, we must fall back to torch.matmul.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Dtype: typical are float32, float16, bfloat16. We’ll accumulate in float32 for all.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Layout: Inputs here are contiguous in the last dimension (row-major). A is (K, M), B is (K, N). We’ll treat them as (M/K, K/N) with strides.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Output: (M, N), row-major contiguous.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The formula C = A.T @ B equals C = (A @ B.T).T, but computing A @ B.T and then transposing costs an extra transpose. Better to compute directly C = A.T @ B.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Kernel structure
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m We’ll implement a blocked matmul:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tile sizes: BLOCK_M x BLOCK_N x BLOCK_K.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Grid: (ceil_div(M, BLOCK_M), ceil_div(N, BLOCK_N)).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Inner loop over K in steps of BLOCK_K.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Loads:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A tile: shape [BLOCK_M, BLOCK_K], from A_T (shape M x K). Addressing uses strides: A_T[offs_m, offs_k].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B tile: shape [BLOCK_K, BLOCK_N], from B (shape K x N). Addressing uses strides: B[offs_k, offs_n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Compute: acc += dot(A_tile, B_tile) -> float32 accumulation.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Store: acc to C[offs_m, offs_n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Memory access and coalescing
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For A_T (M x K): we load along K (fast varying dim) for each m; that’s good if A_T is contiguous along K (it is, because A_T = A.transpose(), and PyTorch preserves contiguous layout; A_T.stride(1) = 1, stride(0) = K). So loads are coalesced along k.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - For B (K x N): we load along N (fast varying dim) for each k; that’s good if B is contiguous along N (B.stride(1) = 1). So loads are coalesced along n.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Overall, this pattern is standard for Triton matmul.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 6) Numerical stability and dtype
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We’ll cast inputs to float32 inside the kernel for accumulation. This is safe and typical. If inputs are half, this improves accuracy. Output will be float32.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - If you need exact dtype matching (e.g., return float16 when inputs are float16), you can cast the result afterward. For simplicity, we return float32.
