[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code does
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The original model’s forward returns torch.matmul(A.T, B).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Shapes:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A: (K, M)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - B: (K, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - A.T: (M, K)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Result C = A.T @ B: shape (M, N)
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - This is a standard GEMM: C[m, n] = sum over k of A_T[m, k] * B[k, n].
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Performance and optimization considerations
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - GEMM is compute- and memory-intensive. PyTorch typically dispatches to cuBLAS/cuBLASLt, which are highly optimized.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Writing a custom Triton matmul kernel can be educational and sometimes competitive, but beating cuBLAS consistently requires deep tuning.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The primary opportunity is to write a correct, blocked, memory-coalesced kernel that:
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Uses reasonable tiling.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Accumulates in float32 for numerical stability.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m   - Avoids illegal memory access and compilation issues.
