[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 1) What the original PyTorch code computes
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - It does a linear layer: y = x @ W.T + b, shape (batch, out_features).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Then it performs x = x * s + original_x which algebraically is just y = y * (1 + s).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The clone+detach and two-step elementwise sequence is redundant; it can be fused into a single scaling.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 2) Where time is spent
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The GEMM x @ W.T dominates runtime.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The elementwise ops are minor but still launch kernels and move memory.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 3) What we did previously
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We wrote a Triton GEMM kernel that computes C = (A @ B) + bias, then scales by (1 + s) in the epilogue. This removed the separate elementwise pass and the clone.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - It achieved a solid speedup (~4.3x on B200 per server feedback).
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 4) Where we can improve further
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Tile and launch configuration tuning: The previous kernel hard-coded BLOCK_M=64, BLOCK_N=64, BLOCK_K=32, num_warps=4, num_stages=3. On B200 and for these problem sizes (M=16384, K=4096, N=4096), larger tiles and more warps often help. Autotuning a small set of configurations lets Triton pick the best for the current shapes/hardware.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Larger BLOCK_K can reduce loop overhead and improve data reuse; BLOCK_K=64 or 128 are often good.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Increase num_warps (e.g., 8) to improve occupancy on B200 for 128x128 tiles.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Use more pipeline stages (num_stages 3–5) to overlap memory and compute.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Keep accumulation in fp32 for numerical stability; keep I/O in fp32 to match the reference and avoid correctness regressions.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m 5) Correctness and numericals
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - The fused epilogue (bias add + scale) is mathematically identical to the original sequence.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - Keeping float32 I/O matches PyTorch’s default and preserves correctness.
[36m(MultiTurnAsyncvLLMEngine pid=1400823)[0m - We still provide a safe fallback to torch if Triton/CUDA is not available.
